.TH AENIGMA-LOCK-KEY 8 "2026-08-12" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-lock-key \- encrypt the Aenigma service's private key with a
passphrase
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-lock-key
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-lock-key
re-encrypts the Aenigma service's RSA private key,
.IR /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key.pem ,
using
.BR openssl (1),
protecting it with a passphrase under
.BR AES\-256 .
It takes no options or arguments.
.PP
The script performs the following actions:
.IP \(bu 2
Reads the existing private key from
.IR /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key.pem .
If the key currently on disk is
.B already
passphrase-protected,
.BR openssl (1)
first prompts for the
.B current
passphrase in order to decrypt it.
.IP \(bu 2
Writes a new
.B AES\-256
encrypted copy of the key to a temporary file,
.IR /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key\-locked.pem ,
prompting interactively for a
.B new
passphrase (and confirmation).
.IP \(bu 2
Replaces the original
.I private\-key.pem
with the newly encrypted file.
.IP \(bu 2
Resets ownership to
.B aenigma:aenigma
and permissions to
.B 700
on the resulting file.
.PP
.B aenigma-lock-key
must be run as root, and requires that
.I private\-key.pem
already exists (typically created beforehand by
.BR aenigma-keys (8)).
.SH OPTIONS
.B aenigma-lock-key
takes no command-line options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The private key was encrypted and stored successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The script was not run as root,
.I private\-key.pem
does not exist, or
.BR openssl (1)
failed (for example, an incorrect current passphrase or an aborted
prompt).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key.pem
The Aenigma service's RSA private key. Read to obtain the key
material, then overwritten in place with the newly encrypted
version. Owned by
.B aenigma:aenigma
with mode
.B 700
after the operation.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key\-locked.pem
Temporary file used to hold the newly encrypted key before it
replaces
.IR private\-key.pem .
Not expected to persist after a successful run.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encrypt the private key, supplying the current passphrase (if any)
and a new one when prompted:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-lock-key
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.BR openssl (1)
is invoked without first checking whether
.I private\-key.pem
is currently encrypted or in plaintext, so the number of passphrase
prompts shown (one for the new passphrase only, or one for the
current passphrase followed by one for the new passphrase) depends
silently on the key's existing state.
.PP
.B aenigma-lock-key
takes no backup of the previous
.I private\-key.pem
before overwriting it. If the new passphrase is lost or mistyped and
confirmed, or if the wrong current passphrase is supplied and the
operation nonetheless proceeds with unexpected results, there is no
built-in way to recover the previous key file's exact prior state
from this script alone.
.PP
The intermediate file
.I private\-key\-locked.pem
is created by
.BR openssl (1)
using the process's default permissions (governed by the calling
shell's umask) and is not restricted to mode
.B 700
until
.B after
it has replaced
.IR private\-key.pem .
Depending on the umask in effect when this script runs, the newly
encrypted key material may be briefly readable by users other than
.B aenigma
between its creation and the final
.BR chmod .
.PP
If the script is interrupted (for example, killed) between writing
.I private\-key\-locked.pem
and the subsequent
.BR mv ,
the temporary file may be left behind on disk and will not be
automatically cleaned up on a later run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openssl (1),
.BR aenigma-keys (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.ruja@aenigma.ro>
